        "math_academy_id": "q-132147",
        "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{2}^{2})$ with $P(X>2)=0.3085,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |",
        "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =1-P(X>2) \\\\  & =1-0.3085 \\\\  & =0.6915\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.6915 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ \u03a6(\\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ \\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & ={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.6915)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(0.5)=0.6915\\,\u21d2\\,{\u03a6}^{-1}(0.6915)=0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & =0.5 \\\\ 2-\\mu  & =1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
